#{[17592186559601 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559601 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559601 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f058d428-0146-5872-86b8-f12ac8d0a722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559704 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "69b45667-9a66-586d-aafa-6664e3584267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559724 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e5650dd-916f-5b6f-b0e5-9eadfbe79952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha $\n\nIII. $A$ might have a complex eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "210e4b47-1e0a-56be-aa39-497b4081eae6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559785 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, we cannot have $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha .$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$\n\nII. $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable\n\nIII. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fb92bee-a19d-5089-88c2-ed5488b77548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559800 #edb/ref 1074 "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00649106-db72-5f98-a26b-cf0c8648ce5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186366636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d29f179-63a2-5b26-9010-60a5f5cccbf2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b5a9152-1cd3-5945-ba4f-3e6468f968f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ac724bf-e8ba-5a05-9ed3-888527594f6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186366650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e00bef3d-8e